Exponential change and logarithmic scales (@ql-logarithms: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @quantitative-literacy, #source @ql-source-algebra, #quantitative-literacy "Geometry-and-volume.lingua#r_6DCACA5A4YM1PC08DT5WEW3BHW", #clean-manufacturing "../../../production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/foundations/Growth-changes-a-contamination-inventory.lingua#r_3C2YBFMP7PGR2VF917VSAEXJXN") { r_0NQPY7MA3SJ3TR97ZJRTW7ZQPG
**Read:** Algebra and Trigonometry 2e chapter 6 completely. Revisit exponential models, logarithm properties, and the distinction between linear, exponential, and logarithmic relationships. Use the graph exercises before taking a fitted line as evidence of a mechanism.

For a constant fractional change per interval, x_n = x₀a^n. The exponent n is dimensionless and the interval must be stated. Multiplying by 0.98 at each of ten stages leaves 0.98^10, about 0.8171, of the original. A repeated loss is not the same as subtracting ten times 2% of a changing denominator.

The argument of a physical logarithm must be a dimensionless ratio, such as ln(x/x_ref). A plot labelled log pressure needs a reference unit or pressure and a stated base. On a log-log plot a power law y = Cx^p becomes a straight line with slope p after choosing consistent references. On a semilog plot an exponential can become straight. Neither straight appearance establishes causation or validity outside the measured interval.

**Work:** compare additive loss, proportional loss, and a fixed loss plus proportional loss for twenty steps. Recover the multiplier from two known values and state what time interval it describes. Plot hypothetical size-and-energy data on ordinary and log axes, derive a candidate exponent, and test its predictions on a point withheld from fitting. Explain why a logarithmic axis cannot display zero and how excluding zeros can conceal a meaningful population.

Revisit these distinctions when a material specification uses orders of magnitude or a later electronics reading uses decibels; the physical definition of each logarithmic quantity still has to be learned.

